okay welcome everyone this is going to  be a relatively short webinar today the  introductory slide there gives you our  standard information maybe one of the  most important things is feel free to  ask questions in the question pod during  the webinar as you think of those  questions we always have the questions  and answers in a materials link on the  courses and webinars page and the  archived webinars just search NCBI  courses and webinars and you'll find  that page and within a few days this  webinar which is being recorded will be  on our YouTube channel along with the  others please feel free to email me  directly if you have questions afterward  Matson at NCBI NLM NIH gov and of course  send general blast questions - last -  help at NCBI domain and sitting here  with me today is Christian Camacho and  Tom Madden developers from the blast  group so you'll get some very expert  answers to your questions Peter Cooper  is on the question pod answering  questions during the webinar so let me  go ahead and give you the take home  message for today so the take-home  message is we highly encourage you to  upgrade to version 2.5 point o before  November 9th and the next slide will  tell you what's going to happen on  November 9th so the first of the topics  today has to do with an ncbi wide switch  to http from standard HTTP but this will  particularly affect standalone blasts in  a couple ways that I'll show you on the  next slide this a change is going to  take effect for api's on November 9th  that deal with blast the general web  pages at NCBI have already made this  change I'll also talk a bit about  another NCBI wide change which is the  phasing out of GI numbers and how using  accession dot version will impact  standalone blast and then very briefly  touch on a couple other improvements  that came out in this release so what's  going to break on November 9th if you  are still using version 2.4 0.0 or  earlier primarily the remote option in  other words if you send searches to our  servers using  the remote option that will fail to work  unless you have upgraded to two point  five point Oh on November 9th another  function some of you may or may not be  using but it's very handy and that is a  pre fetch function in the past in other  words in previous versions you could  only use that with the remote option but  in this version it's been improved and  you can use that without the remote  option so what happens is you input a  query file that contains identifiers  either gi's or accessions and the  programs will fetch those IDs from NCBI  to get the sequence records so you will  not be able to use these two functions  if you have not upgraded before November  9 now having said that we plan to have a  test this coming Thursday October 13th  from 10:00 a.m. to 11:00 a.m. Eastern  Time in other words you know try running  version 2.4 point O and it should not  work during that time but two-point 5.0  should we may very well have other tests  before the ninth we probably will before  November 9 and look on our webpages for  an announcement of those tests hopefully  we can get an announcement out in the  blast announce email list for those  other upcoming tests ok so the second  major change has to do with phasing out  of GIS and I've got a couple links on  the last couple slides where you can get  more general information about both the  HTTP change and the phase-out of GIS but  in terms of standalone blast what's  going to happen is you'll be seeing more  and more the new style of definition  line versus the old style so in the old  style definition line starts with GI and  then a pipe symbol and then the number  and so on we're moving now to just what  we call bear accession dot version as  you see in the new style now those of  you who are downloading our fast a  databases from the blast FTP site have  already noticed this  we've already switched over to the  accession dot version definition lines  but there are other places where this  will become more apparent in standalone  blast for example and  tbh command if you retrieve sequences  from our pre-formatted databases that is  those one directory up from fast a the  default setting for returning fast day  will fit out the new style format in  other words accession dot version you  may see on my example command line here  I've used an accession you can still use  GI numbers as input to Blas TB command  but you'll get back  by default this accession dot version  format some other changes and make Blas  TB it's been improved so it will accept  both the old and new style definition  lines so you don't have to worry about  changing everything just yet  it will also guess the source from the  accession alone if you're using parsec  IDs and I assume that would be primarily  with our pre formatted databases also  many of you use the GI list option to  create a custom database say to an  organism or taxonomic group that GI  lists which is still available and will  remain available but we also have a seek  ID list option so that seek ID list  option will take the accession dot  versions it's a bit slow with large  lists right now but that's going to be  improved in future releases we hope by  the next release if not the one  following that the next release by the  way is to point 6.0 and as of today  that's scheduled to come out either in  December of this year perhaps early next  year ok couple other changes in the  default output format so those of you  using the tabular output formats you're  now going to get by default query  accession sequence accession as shown  here in other words the accession  numbers in the next release you'll be  getting accession dodging so you will  not be getting back by default the GI  style definition lines ok so that's  those are the two major changes one of  the improvements we've made is to offer  another output format option and that's  output  format option 18 will now give you a  taxonomy organism report so if you're  familiar with using this on the web it's  similar and look to that very nicely  organizes the output by organism as you  see in this example and finally in terms  of improvements we now have included  composition based statistics when you  use RPS T blast n you're not familiar  with that program this allows you to  input a nucleotide sequence it does the  translations and searches against the  conserved domain database you get back  conserved domains not bet back  RPS blast as some of you may know allows  you input a protein sequence and see  what conserved domains are there our PST  blast n allows you to input a nucleotide  sequence ok so I promised a couple links  so you can go to our news page to find  out about the general GI number  phase-out and go to our home page in the  developed section and then the API  section you can read about the switch to  HTTPS and for full discussion of the  release notes go to the NCBI bookshelf  you can just search NCBI bookshelf blast  and you'll get these release notes  written by Christiaan so I'm going to  open this up for any questions I'm gonna  check out our chat pod here see if we've  had any questions come in ok we're not  seeing any questions at the moment thank  you very much and we will see you at the  next webinar  you
 

